-- title:
--   Lemma D.1.4.4 — $G\cong\varprojlim_{H\in\mathrm{CG}(\widehat G)}\widehat H$: a locally compact abelian group is the inverse limit of the duals of the open compactly generated subgroups of $\widehat G$
-- statement:
--   **Approximation of a locally compact abelian group by locally Euclidean groups** (Lemma D.1.4.4 of the source).
--
--   Let $G$ be a Hausdorff locally compact abelian group with Pontryagin dual $\widehat G$, and let $\mathrm{CG}(\widehat G)$ be the set of all open compactly generated subgroups of $\widehat G$ (open subgroups generated by a compact neighbourhood of the identity), ordered by inclusion. For $H\subseteq J$ in $\mathrm{CG}(\widehat G)$, restriction of characters is a continuous homomorphism $\widehat J\to\widehat H$, and these maps form an inverse system with limit
--   $$\varprojlim_{H\in\mathrm{CG}(\widehat G)}\widehat H=\Big\{(a_H)_H\in\prod_{H\in\mathrm{CG}(\widehat G)}\widehat H\ :\ a_J|_H=a_H\ \text{whenever }H\subseteq J\Big\},$$
--   a subgroup of the product with the subspace topology. Every $x\in G$ defines a compatible family: $a_H(\chi)=\chi(x)$ for $\chi\in H\subseteq\widehat G$. The lemma states that the resulting canonical continuous homomorphism
--   $$\gamma\colon G\longrightarrow\varprojlim_{H\in\mathrm{CG}(\widehat G)}\widehat H\qquad\text{(D.1.4.4)}$$
--   is an isomorphism of topological groups.
--
--   Since each $\widehat H$ is locally Euclidean (Theorem D.1.4.3(1)), every Hausdorff locally compact abelian group is canonically a cofiltered inverse limit of locally Euclidean groups. The source uses this presentation in (D.1.4.8)–(D.1.4.16) and Lemma D.1.4.7 to describe $G$ through elementary abelian Lie groups, and thereby to define the Schwartz–Bruhat space $\mathcal S(G)$ as a locally convex inductive limit, following Bruhat.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The index set is the subtype of subgroups `H` of `PontryaginDual G` with `IsOpen (H : Set _) ∧ ∃ U ∈ 𝓝 1, IsCompact U ∧ Subgroup.closure U = H` (for an open subgroup this is compact generation of $H$ in the sense of Definition D.1.4.1). The statement asserts the existence of a continuous homomorphism `γ` from `G` to the product `(H : I) → PontryaginDual H` with `γ x H χ = χ x`, and expresses "isomorphism onto the inverse limit" by two conditions: `IsEmbedding γ` (`γ` is a homeomorphism onto its image with the subspace topology of the product) and `Set.range γ` is exactly the set of compatible families, compatibility being `a H χ = a J (Subgroup.inclusion h χ)` for `h : H ≤ J`.
-- source:
--   A. M. Masullo, The Music of the Primes, draft of 20 November 2026, https://math216.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/the-music-of-the-primes.pdf, p. 758, Lemma D.1.4.4, eq. (D.1.4.4)

import Mathlib

open Topology

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace LCAGroup

theorem equiv_inverseLimit_dual_openCompactlyGenerated
    (G : Type*) [CommGroup G] [TopologicalSpace G] [IsTopologicalGroup G]
    [T2Space G] [LocallyCompactSpace G] :
    ∃ γ : G →ₜ* ((H : {H : Subgroup (PontryaginDual G) //
          IsOpen (H : Set (PontryaginDual G)) ∧
            ∃ U ∈ 𝓝 (1 : PontryaginDual G), IsCompact U ∧ Subgroup.closure U = H}) →
        PontryaginDual H.1),
      (∀ (x : G) H (χ : H.1), γ x H χ = (χ : PontryaginDual G) x) ∧
      IsEmbedding γ ∧
      Set.range γ = {a | ∀ H J (h : H.1 ≤ J.1) (χ : H.1),
        a H χ = a J (Subgroup.inclusion h χ)} := by sorry

end LCAGroup
